    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Layering is the art of wearing complementary scents so they read as a harmonious, single composition. The goal is not to fog the room with competing notes; it is to create nuance, depth, and contrast.</p><p>There is a fundamental chemistry to successful layering: heavy, resinous scents form the foundation, while light, sparkling notes provide the top accent. Here are the simple rules that unlock infinite possibilities.</p>"
      }
    },
    "blocks_ji7beH": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "blog_editorial_text_section_zNd6WG": {
          "type": "blog--editorial-text-section",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The Golden Rules: Heavy Anchors First, Light Accents Second",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_size_mobile": 28,
            "heading_line_height": 1.1,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_margin_bottom": 30,
            "heading_margin_bottom_mobile": 25,
            "column_1_text": "<p>The foundational rule of fragrance layering is strictly molecular: always apply the heaviest fragrance first. Scents rich in oud, sandalwood, amber, vanilla, or leather have heavy molecular weights that cling tightly to the skin.</p><p>If you spray a light citrus cologne first and bury it under a heavy amber Extrait, you will suffocate the delicate citrus molecules before they have a chance to project.</p>",
            "column_2_text": "<p>Once your heavy anchor has settled onto skin for sixty seconds, apply your lighter accent fragrance directly over it. Fresh florals, sparkling citrus, and airy aromatics will float effortlessly above the rich base.</p><p>The result is a dynamic 3-dimensional scent cloud that evolves uniquely throughout the day as your body temperature fluctuates.</p>",
            "body_size": 17,
            "body_size_mobile": 15,
            "body_line_height": 1.9,
            "body_color": "#2D2A26",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "column_gap": 55,
            "column_gap_mobile": 30,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "padding_top": 60,
            "padding_bottom": 60,
            "padding_horizontal": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 40,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 40,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "blog_editorial_text_section_zNd6WG"
      ],
      "name": "Editorial text section",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "1778690749e24fd023": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "blog_narrative_content_9nTtAA": {
          "type": "blog--narrative-content",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "section_padding": 60,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "heading": "<p>Master layering combos: Addiction & Fierce</p>",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_spacing": 25,
            "box_color": "#1C1917",
            "box_radius": 15,
            "box_padding": 45,
            "box_spacing": 35,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>One of our absolute favorite layering recipes combines the crisp, spicy bergamot of <strong>Fierce</strong> (inspired by Sauvage Parfum) with the sweet saffron and amberwood warmth of <strong>Addiction</strong> (inspired by Baccarat Rouge 540).</p><p>The pepper and ambroxan in Fierce temper the sweetness of Addiction, while the saffron adds an opulent, ultra-luxury texture to the clean woods. It is an intoxicating combination that smells like a $600 bespoke niche creation.</p><p>Another winning duo is pairing <strong>Marine</strong> with <strong>Sweet Cherry</strong> for a surprising clash of ocean breeze and tart almond liqueur.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#F5EFE6",
            "box_text_size": 17,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.9,
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "subheading": "<p>Mistakes worth avoiding</p>",
            "subheading_size": 42,
            "subheading_color": "#1A1816",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>The biggest pitfall is layering two high-projection, complex powerhouses together—such as combining two heavy ouds or competing gourmands. That creates olfactory chaos and clears rooms.</p><p>Keep one fragrance simple and focused (like a clean cedar or vanilla) and let the second fragrance provide the complexity.</p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 17,
            "bottom_text_color": "#2D2A26",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.9
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "blog_narrative_content_9nTtAA"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
